   "id": "db10366e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## I. Fractional Bounded Knapsack\n",
    "\n",
    "In the fractional version of the problem, you are allowed to take arbitrary fractions of items.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Q1.** Write a function `knapfrac(values, weights, quantities, capacity)` whose arguments are:\n",
    "- a list of item values,\n",
    "- a list of item weights,\n",
    "- a list of upper bounds on the quantities of each item,\n",
    "- an integer capacity (the maximum weight the knapsack can hold),\n",
    "\n",
    "and which returns the maximum value of a fractional knapsack."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 39,
   "id": "cf6e967d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
   "def knapfrac(values, weights, quantities, capacity):\n",
   "\n",
   "    n = len(values)\n",
   "\n",
   "    # We first compute the ratios, in order to determine which item is the most profitable per unit of weight\n",
   "    ratios = []\n",
   "    for i in range(n):\n",
   "        ratios.append(values[i] / weights[i])\n",
   "\n",
   "    # We order the index of the items in decreasing order of ratios\n",
   "    items_sorted_by_ratio_index = sorted(range(n), key=lambda i: ratios[i], reverse=True)\n",
   "\n",
   "    # Now we take items in the order computed previously\n",
   "    total_sum = 0\n",
   "\n",
   "    for i in items_sorted_by_ratio_index:\n",
   "        quantity_added = min(quantities[i], capacity / weights[i])\n",
   "\n",
   "        total_sum += values[i] * quantity_added\n",
   "        capacity -= weights[i] * quantity_added\n",
   "\n",
   "        if capacity == 0:\n",
   "            break\n",
   "\n",
   "    return total_sum"
  ]
  },
