    "## II. Bounded Knapsack by Dynamic Programming\n",
    "\n",
    "**Q1.** Provide and explain a recurrence relation satisfied by the bounded knapsack problem."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "537c7e58",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Answer:** \n",
    "\n",
    "*[ thoughts, i will leave a trace of my reasoning steps and what i have tried]:*\n",
    "*I need to be sure of the defenition of the bounded problem, here i assume it just like the fractional one but the decision variables (number of items i choose per item) is a natural number instead of a real number. my decision variables will be called X_i (i because one per item).*\n",
    "\n",
    "We are then working on maximixing the following function: **SUM(i=1 to n)_{ X_i * v_i }**\n",
    "While these constraints are true:\n",
    "\n",
    "- for all i :  **0 <= X_i <= b-i** \n",
    "- **SUM(i=1 to n)_{ X_i * w_i } <= W**\n",
    "- for all i : X_i is in the natural number set\n",
    "\n",
    "This exercice asks us to work in Dynamic Programming so we need to find a recursive relation in this problem. My first instict is to recurse on the number of items, it seems like the only parameter we can recurse with maybe the other being Weight. I will choose to explore by the number of items (fixing W to its base value).\n",
    "\n",
    "- After working on the problem my it seems more easy to recurse on the Weights. because if i know the best combinations for W=1 to 4 then for W=5 i have to only check the new bigger items and the combinations of 1+4 and 2+3 to see wich is better. \n",
    "\n",
    "the above approach does not seem to work because of the bounded values, in theory it works but we need to keep a lot of information between the recursive steps, i will search for something else.\n",
    "\n",
    "Well the idea may be as simple as to recurse on 2 parameters, the number of items and i, i recurse on the weight used by each item, that would be the same as to track X_i or to recurse on W too. \n",
    "In class we saw the Rod problem, we can use a similar analogy, i suppose that i alredy know the best value cases for my previous \"smaller cuts\" of the rod, then i check all the possibles values X_i (named k_j) for the new i-th item and for each fill the reminder r_j of the rod r_j = (W - X_i*k_j) with the best case that i already know for that \"lenght\" thus completing the recursive relation without having to pass the bi information backwards since it is solved independently at each step.\n",
    "\n",
    "The complexity is very direct to calculate, we have to check the max between the at worst bi + 1 values for each then we have to that for each Weight. So the complexity comes at\n",
    "\n",
    "**O(W * SUM(i=1 to n){ bi + 1})**\n",
    "\n",
    "---------\n",
    "\n",
    "I asked the Ai gemini about it thoughts about my solution (you can find in annex at the end of the subject the prompt) it proposed 2 things: a way to reduce complexity by calculating the solutions for the powers of 2 and using the base 2 to reduce complexity to \n",
    "\n",
    "**O(W * SUM(i=1 to n){ log(bi) } )**\n",
    "\n",
    "it also proposed a new solution it named: Sliding Window DP, that is even better using the fact that we also care about the reminders (modulo) of the w_i, since we only transition from one state to another if the 'hole' left by the cut is exactly a size of a multiple of the weight. managing to reduce the complexity to \n",
    "\n",
    "**O(W*n)**\n",
